A list of symbols is an application
===================================

Seam: a Python list whose first element is a symbol is an application, so
a list of constructors or of functions cannot be spelled directly.  Issue
#104, open: this driver pins today's output.  Memo: A1 (a list is a list,
M.f(x) is the application).  Catalogue: P2, P44 (the errors name the
symptom, not the rule).
Output today: countItems [A, B] is an ArityError on A, and applyAll
[inc, dbl] 5 is a MismatchError on the argument of inc.  The escapes run:
[[A], [B]] and [curry.expr(A), B] count 2, and applyAll [[inc], [dbl]] 5
is [6, 10]; [A] alone is the value A, not a list.  A tuple is never an
application, so typeof (A, B) is a pair of Item.  The rule at work:
[inc, 1] is the application inc 1, which prints as 'inc 1' and is 2.
A fixed system prints 2 for countItems [A, B] and [6, 10] for applyAll
[inc, dbl] 5 (the first two lines of expected.out change); the [inc, 1]
lines would then build a list and fail at the type of its elements (a
reading of memo A1, not a decision).
No oracle: the goals are the spellings of the Python boundary.
Backends: py and cxx, one output.
